From: Oliver Seiler <oseiler@unixg.ubc.ca>
Date: Tue, 1 Nov 1994 13:37:38 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-11-11] Anti-Statism Not Consistent with Boundless Expansion

On Mon, 31 Oct 1994 nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu wrote:

>  Oliver Seiler <oseiler@unixg.ubc.ca> wrote:
> >
> >On Wed, 26 Oct 1994, Kobielus, James wrote:
> >
> >What does bureaucracy have to do with boundless expansion. By definition,
> >nothing at all. Take something like nanotechnology. At this point I'd
> >have to say that most research in this area is being pursued at government
> >and corporate funded institutions. But this is just money. The real work is
> >being done by individuals putting some brain power into solving the problems
> >associated with nanotech. As in the semiconductor industry, only a few small
> >breakthroughs are necessary to realize nanotech as a product.
> 
> Bureaucracy is an interesting question. At this point (as the owner of
> a *very* small business), I've come to believe that a certain amount
> of record-keeping is necessary for projects which are too large or
> which have too many boring details for one person to keep in mind.

I should explain myself more on this point. Record-keeping and project
management are two things that I think are necessary and quite useful.
However, like all good things, more of a good thing isn't necessarily
better. My rule of thumb is to try to find the optimal balance of
record-keeping, management, etc. to move an individual project (since
I am a project-based person) forward. To me a bureaucracy is an
organization which doesn't address the optimization issue. My general
feeling is that these organizations eventually stagnate and die, as
the major amount of energy isn't spent on "useful" work, but on
"infrastructure" work...

> Is part of boundless expansion finding ways to either do without
> most records and/or to hand record-keeping off to computers?

As this is an extension of technology which allows humankind to
work (and play) more efficiently, I would say this could be a large
part of boundless expansion...
 
> Is there an advantage to cultures (common in the far east, I understand)
> where it's normal to have major contracts be verbal and people are
> expected to keep the spirit of the contract?

If more people understood non zero-sum game theory and it's implications
on social behavior, then I think it has advantages...

> >> a state apparatus unlike any in human experience.  Scattered ideamongers, 
> >> code cutters, and gene splicers won't make it all happen.
> >
> >And guys sitting in offices, middle managers, supervisors, and other waste
> >organics won't do it for sure. The smart money is on the individuals coming
> >up with ideas.
> 
> As I said above, I'm not convinced that the guys in offices, middle
> managers, etc. actually *are* waste organics. They don't invent the
> new ideas, but the new ideas might be getting very limited application
> if it weren't for the people who mass produce and transmit them.

I was being somewhat snide. I know from experience that management is
needed for project completion. I do know that without the creative
people working under the guidance of the manager (and although this
sounds like a hierarchical arrangment, management is a flexible and
dynamic thing, or at least should be...) then projects won't move
forward either.

-Oliver

