From: fcp@nuance.com (Craig Presson)
Date: Mon, 31 Oct 1994 06:22:21 -0600
Subject: [#94-11-16] Argument by Analogy (Upload)

At 03:07 PM 11/1/94 EST, Reilly Jones wrote:
[...]
>I am not an elaborate machine, and my personality and enterprises have not been
>implemented.
>
>I am that I am.

Oh, I see, now you're the old testament God (or his doubtful
messenger Descartes?). Non serviam.

[...]
[...]>have no more rights than a toaster, that is, none.

Aha, now we see why you feel threatened. A long time ago we had a 
discussion here on "rights" and someone(s), including Perry Metzger
if I remember correctly, made a very good case that "rights" make
a poor leg to stand on, and that contracts are much better. Sadly,
I don't remember enough of that argument (anyone else?), but even
with lost details, it serves to point out the difference between
you and a toaster, at least -- the toaster cannot make and defend
claims or enter into and perform contracts.

"Free will" does not distinguish the class of machines from the class
of humans, since I can demonstrate simple non-deterministic machines, and
even, in the computer, evolved machines; one of those that was non-d and
evolved might well not wish to be called an artifact anymore (Minsky and
Harrison's Sven [in _The Turing Option_] insisted on being called an
MI for "machine intellligence" instead of an AI).

  This designation of 
>'machine' is not a frivolous matter.  It is grown-up (meaning post-doctorate)
>philosophy, not Ph 101.

It is Ph 101 in the sense that it is a very fundamental question
that philosophy does not have the tools to handle, which is
why you see it as a grown-up question. Philosophy
as an enterprise is in shambles. Philosophers can't even agree on
a philosophy of science, even while scientists have achieved rings
around them for 374 years.

[...]
>  Philosophers know this, especially ones that have
>had children, all you have to do is tell humanity, "No, you can't
> do that!" and you have instilled a huge desire.

Sorry, I will not be treated like a child by philosophers or anyone
else. We've outgrown it -- George Boole delivered the coup de grace
100 years ago, by mathematizing the last useful bit of philosophy. 

Show me one thing a philosopher has accomplished in his subject in the
last 50 years. To me, they seem mostly to trot along beside the scientists
and engineers and shout warnings. I am already wearing my mittens, thanks.

 






